steps.prompt:
These are calendar events, each with a row number:
{events}

For each event pick:
calendar: exactly one calendar name from the calendars table. Use this priority: a trip whose point is the journey itself (a train, flight or drive from one place to another) goes on the calendar whose use is travel, but an activity that happens on the move or at the other end (a bike ride, a workshop, a trip to the dentist) goes on that activity's calendar; else an activity keyword (e.g. gym, dentist) wins; else the company or work it is for; else the calendar of the person it is with; else IU. Anything with Martin (the boss) or for the client goes on Work, whatever the activity. Sport with friends goes on Friends. A place never decides the calendar, only the activity and the people do, but a family member's home counts as being with them (dinner at Mum's is Family). The trip rule comes before all others. Always pick exactly one calendar.
people: for every name in who, the exact text of the person column of the people table, with the brackets, e.g. "Martin" (only people in who, never add anyone; if a name fits two rows of the people table, the work one only when the event is work, else the other one). If a name is not in the table, copy it as said.

Calendars (calendar,keywords,use) and people (person,also_written_as,email,calendar):
{knowledge}

Answer as JSON {"picks": [{"row": 1, "calendar": "...", "people": ["..."]}]}
